//
// A `for` loop's variable is an ordinary FUNCTION-scoped local: it
// outlives its loop, and a second loop over a different element type
// in the same function is a compile ERROR.
//
// The error is correct and well-worded — it is loft#690's fix, which
// turned a silent corruption into a diagnostic, and that half is
// closed.  What is left is the SCOPE: `i` in `for i in …` is not the
// loop's, so two unrelated loops in one function share a name.
//
// ── What it costs a consumer ────────────────────────────────────
//
// dryopea gave up on short loop variables entirely.  122 of the 131
// `for` loops in `src/` carry a per-FUNCTION prefix — `wt_i`, `wd_e`,
// `ent_t`, `tslr_w`, `asm_e` — and `CLAUDE.md` § Naming records the
// rule as a language workaround:
//
//     Loop variables prefixed per function (`tslr_w`, `tpi_pal`)
//     to dodge the flat-namespace gotcha
//
// The prefix is not readability; it is collision avoidance, invented
// once per function, and it is what a reader meets first in every
// loop in the project.  A loop-scoped variable would delete the
// convention outright.
//
// ⚠ It also bites in the direction the error cannot help with: adding
// a second loop to a long function can fail to compile because of a
// name a hundred lines away, and the fix is to rename the NEW loop.
//
// Expected output:
//     error: loop variable 'i' has type integer but was previously used as text
//
// ⚠ Delete the `two_types` FUNCTION to see the controls — commenting
// out the CALL is not enough, because the clash is a compile error.
// The rest then runs and prints:
//     0 1 0 1 0 1    <- the same name at the same type is fine
//     leaked i = 1   <- and the variable outlives its loop

fn same_type_twice() {
    // Two loops, one name, one type: legal.  So the clash is by TYPE,
    // not by name — which is what makes it surprising.
    for i in 0..2 { println("{i}"); }
    for i in 0..2 { println("{i}"); }
}

fn outlives_its_loop() {
    for i in 0..2 { println("{i}"); }
    // The loop is over and `i` is still in scope, holding its last
    // value.  This is the scope the error above is enforcing.
    println("leaked i = {i}");
}

fn two_types() {
    for i in ["a", "b"] { println("{i}"); }
    // error: loop variable 'i' has type integer but was previously
    //        used as text
    for i in 0..2 { println("{i}"); }
}

fn main() {
    same_type_twice();
    outlives_its_loop();
    two_types();
}
